{"kind":"report","text":"Changes requested on the complete 157-subject I1 source package at exact 34d3b65664072b147c3967d3fd7620c841743985, primary delivery artifact 3e9352999d6089ee3e01e183092c1f35ffacdb39, under invitation 8ed4f88d and checker promise 48c70a06. Compared with the previously completely reviewed 97518f68eaae88591a1aead854d69023577c1268. This is the ordinary whole-package review, not a replacement by preliminary 6362250d. Two P2 corrections remain in the existing 5fc07d88 / 2d706f18 repair scope.\n\n[P2] Validate the actor as the contract's KeyId in the shared fixed-record guard. packages/bytes/src/records.ts109 uses text for Intent.actor, while contract/src/intent.ts32 declares KeyId and contract/src/scope.ts18–19 specifies its form. An otherwise passing act entry with intent.actor \"alice\" passes isIntent/isSignedIntentShape/isInput/isEntry and therefore the client's history/entry response guard at answers.ts61–62 on both HTTP and service-binding paths. This is not a value of the declared fixed type. isKeyId is already imported and used for Grant.key at records.ts115; reuse it for actor and extend the existing nested-answer table to retain this invariant. Delivery59–71 and delta154/579 should not claim every identifier complete until this is corrected. This is a response/record shape defect, not an authentication bypass: judge.ts80 checks verifySignedIntent, and sign.ts18–20/34–40 rejects malformed key IDs.\n\n[P2] Stop owned stream reading and retention when the exchange expires. takeBytes at packages/bytes/src/take.ts17–34 receives no cancellation state. within47–54 races the callback and aborts only the signal supplied to Fetch. Both exposed Fetch contracts permit a structurally conforming implementation that ignores that signal (client/http.ts26; replay/source.ts71). If a pending read resolves with a late under-limit chunk after the caller receives LATE, takeBytes still retains it and issues another read, and can later join the chunks. Replay's